#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096412 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a34b79-65ff-5f7b-a8ee-5f19424fd9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Given its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "c34ce110-10f4-5bee-97ac-bc4209c6938e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5094" :question/problem "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1125/Source/Images/025bf6fd6149dfc55da76e1dbb72e3eb.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is downward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0)$, and passes through the point $(6,-4)$.\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$, the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\nx^2 = 4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(6,-4)$, so we can substitute $x = 6$ and $y = -4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4py \\\\\n6^{2} &= 4p(-4) \\\\\n36 &= -16p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{9}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4py \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{9}{4})y \\\\\nx^{2} &= -9y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096413 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "281514e9-6e16-5b21-ba4e-503991d811c2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "291b9880-227d-5d7a-b3b0-5f933abeb8fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-5095" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(2,-4)$." :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$, the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\nx^2 = 4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,-4)$, so we can substitute $x = 2$ and $y = -4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4py \\\\\n2^{2} &= 4p(-4) \\\\\n4 &= -16p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4py \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{1}{4})y \\\\\nx^{2} &= -y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096414 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "5ef63d62-a163-52ea-b92f-c1e0131e9c5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5096" :question/problem "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1125/Source/Images/d522d0281a2935fc62efdddf7cdf5df0.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is downward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,4)$, and passes through the point $(4,2)$.\n\nWe know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k)$, the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k)\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (0,4)\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\nx^2 = 4p(y-4)\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p$. We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,2)$, so we can substitute $x = 4$ and $y = 2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4p(y - 4) \\\\\n4^{2} &= 4p(2 - 4) \\\\\n16 &= -8p \\\\\np &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4p(y - 4) \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4(-2)(y - 4) \\\\\nx^{2} &= -8(y - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096415 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "c62e52ff-4940-5529-9664-78636bfaaa7c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5097" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,-2)$ and which passes through the point $(4,6)$." :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k)$, the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k)\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (0,-2)\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 0)^{2} &= 4p(y - (-2)) \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4p(y + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p$. We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,6)$, so we can substitute $x = 4$ and $y = 6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4p(y + 2) \\\\\n(4)^{2} &= 4p(6 + 2) \\\\\n16 &= 32p \\\\\np &= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} &= 4p(y + 2) \\\\\nx^{2} &= 4(\\frac{1}{2})(y + 2) \\\\\nx^{2} &= 2(y + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a34b79-65ff-5f7b-a8ee-5f19424fd9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "975bd0b4-681c-55a1-8f6c-6ce36fed2c39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266171} {:answer/id #uuid "b9151107-51c2-5e79-a235-18d1d54b4f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266172} {:answer/id #uuid "520bddcc-7de9-5c34-8169-4b3872cba3a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266173} {:answer/id #uuid "990397f4-d2c4-5fb2-8870-d1feed525c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266174} {:answer/id #uuid "34d473f3-8e21-57f2-8b27-24896af5998f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "990397f4-d2c4-5fb2-8870-d1feed525c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266174} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba3a9320-0a9e-5b7a-8e31-95cffb38d468" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266170}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d68b304-5abf-5a45-9d18-e5b77fc90d3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115415" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561/assets/52b283fd5a8d9f374fad0a007f127598b86c53d7acf4df05d1415589e43faaa0.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is upward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(3,1).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(3,1),$ so we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {3}^{2} & =4p(1) \\\\ 9 & =4p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a34b79-65ff-5f7b-a8ee-5f19424fd9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02823bdb-2660-5da8-877e-f9364c6e0aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{2}{25}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266178} {:answer/id #uuid "43e659d6-1997-531b-b9d9-ccc896447f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{25}{8}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266179} {:answer/id #uuid "8f5933a3-9027-5a6d-acb7-78429e5e55f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{5}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266180} {:answer/id #uuid "6894233a-2b99-5b0f-8382-a32d45667665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{25}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266181} {:answer/id #uuid "9915f9d2-bef4-56e2-9d8d-7e2a563750ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{5}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6894233a-2b99-5b0f-8382-a32d45667665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{25}{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266181} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f1ffe50-4d3f-5030-a36b-d17de374dadc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266177}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a77e9cba-1e54-5206-88b6-f1eec27273c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-115420" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561/assets/410da7fdbf283aba57c2000a37c56a87e11ed1b1372183a1d3c808376dba495f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is upward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(-5,2).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-5,2),$ so we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-5)^{2} & =4p(2) \\\\ 25 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{25}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{25}{8})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266183 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "281514e9-6e16-5b21-ba4e-503991d811c2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47ca1c1e-e834-5998-91a4-10d42cf3ef55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266185} {:answer/id #uuid "99f9bbd2-cf8d-5e89-9ccd-953346f2b165" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=16y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266186} {:answer/id #uuid "728bb18e-8de9-5eb5-ae95-9c708f7b67d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266187} {:answer/id #uuid "686428a3-f0cd-5673-8075-592202cb5ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266188} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ae281d-c232-51a2-9e5e-e7cb425ace03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "686428a3-f0cd-5673-8075-592202cb5ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266188} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2e94856-8e65-5e4c-a8d9-1083a6e7a5bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266184}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "71cb5f41-13a9-5133-a665-cd67b9feb099" :question/math-academy-id "q-115432" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,1),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(1) \\\\ 4 & =4p \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(1)y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266190 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "281514e9-6e16-5b21-ba4e-503991d811c2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5838fbe-b90a-580e-b8a9-c21146e69279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266192} {:answer/id #uuid "5491da2a-e56b-5a17-86ec-3b184df4a7bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266193} {:answer/id #uuid "6c782733-ebeb-5956-9c17-d28e8f15d7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266194} {:answer/id #uuid "ee0d5dd8-367f-5471-93f2-9d7a1499a78a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266195} {:answer/id #uuid "b72728c4-b12e-5c86-80e4-c7870bdecbd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c782733-ebeb-5956-9c17-d28e8f15d7d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266194} :answer-field/id #uuid "632b4391-f188-577c-b273-c8a2542a0d56" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266191}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33949929-48a3-573a-95fa-dbf4e9614d41" :question/math-academy-id "q-115435" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,-1),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=-1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-1)^{2} & =4p(-1) \\\\ 1 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69727341-5395-5439-876e-81c9187b0654" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266199} {:answer/id #uuid "d8394802-2c1c-5dd2-8e17-8223ce180722" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=2(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266200} {:answer/id #uuid "898831f4-09ed-5bbe-a7f1-be49ad568777" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=4(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266201} {:answer/id #uuid "91f187db-7f2c-5f24-94cd-1ac4b2f78ed0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266202} {:answer/id #uuid "a352c2c6-b919-561c-a9da-99b051b6cfac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8394802-2c1c-5dd2-8e17-8223ce180722" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=2(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266200} :answer-field/id #uuid "70fe7882-011f-57aa-af16-b57c2078fc13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266198}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "999f624c-8ca7-5be0-b050-cb183cb66954" :question/math-academy-id "q-115451" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561/assets/970db64a0ddd325993323511ad4ae754ce64f64d591877bfa0ac908874e2393f.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-1).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,3),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (4-2)^{2} & =4p(3-1) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =2(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "809f1619-f4e7-54ab-b843-b8e97daddc2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{9}{16}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266206} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5d8b61-1506-5240-a544-b80b834e687a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266207} {:answer/id #uuid "fc48066d-1956-593c-9fd2-1cfd566e9b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266208} {:answer/id #uuid "f9cb6175-01c7-5464-8a04-fee3f1f21585" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266209} {:answer/id #uuid "676ef7ca-5c4e-51ce-b0b1-3941477a891f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc48066d-1956-593c-9fd2-1cfd566e9b50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266208} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1542fa1-2577-538b-9e22-2c6387a913dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266205}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6d56f05c-64ef-5ac1-bc49-3c55d8d294be" :question/math-academy-id "q-115500" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561/assets/e26f3115e64d22d336c164fca762a1654ce515019f425f5b0b772276ca88f727.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4py.\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,4),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (1-4)^{2} & =4p(4) \\\\ 9 & =16p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{16})y \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f30cb5ae-bf96-57a0-a6cf-42c06b900390" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{5}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266213} {:answer/id #uuid "2bfb46ce-dbfa-5a32-a9ba-74872fa18636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{5}(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266214} {:answer/id #uuid "d9eb3a40-2e6e-5539-88c8-c3c955073b0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-12(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266215} {:answer/id #uuid "5231be61-4e16-55f3-adb4-192d8bd88c63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-3(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266216} {:answer/id #uuid "c6229a1e-49f5-52c5-ad11-0e6d36098d47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+2)^{2}=12(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9eb3a40-2e6e-5539-88c8-c3c955073b0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-12(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266215} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cdf3938-ca47-51f6-927c-d5d12bf10f98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266212}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "937d704e-62e1-5a63-bbea-5651cad7c9e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-115502" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561/assets/c6b336b55e48a68dcad83efc4a8acd7659225b8f8e29faf8ea176c2cead29901.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-1).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-4,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (-4-2)^{2} & =4p(-2-1) \\\\ 36 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(-3)(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-12(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d5e5e32-ef6c-50ba-a566-989968bbd42a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=3(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266220} {:answer/id #uuid "c385a7ce-f971-566a-8301-9f8c64441141" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=3(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266221} {:answer/id #uuid "4a03f5af-a439-518b-a185-b726f300f22b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}(y-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266222} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf43513-9bd0-5686-b55b-0ff1287de324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{28}(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266223} {:answer/id #uuid "5fff1346-1826-52a9-9779-84248e9702c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=\\frac{4}{28}(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d5e5e32-ef6c-50ba-a566-989968bbd42a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=3(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266220} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5d4a420-f7be-5b78-b182-15152b620b4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266219}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85066d46-803d-56c3-bb8d-ae8f69048626" :question/math-academy-id "q-115506" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1,-5)$ and which passes through the point $(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-1,-5).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-1))^{2} & =4p(y-(-5)) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(2,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (2+1)^{2} & =4p(-2+5) \\\\ 9 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})(y+5) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =3(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7579e360-adab-571e-a192-6bde16a58b76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266227} {:answer/id #uuid "c10646da-ad94-5fd6-beea-c3a493653a6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=5(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266228} {:answer/id #uuid "02b6110d-f957-54b1-9813-613e3460134c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266229} {:answer/id #uuid "4dd91976-05bb-5e0d-ac65-8e431ef525cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266230} {:answer/id #uuid "a3822f1c-ab06-5fcc-82e8-3c95ca604ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=\\frac{1}{5}(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4dd91976-05bb-5e0d-ac65-8e431ef525cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=\\frac{4}{5}(y+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266230} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c33ae47-a099-595a-94ef-bf98016f8619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266226}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "813942f5-d8d5-574a-a4f8-125c265b93a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-115509" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-3)$ and which passes through the point $(-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(1,-3).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-(-3)) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-1,2),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+3) \\\\ (-1-1)^{2} & =4p(2+3) \\\\ 4 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{5})(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb41fbf9-fb94-555b-a4f0-b34bf3bed3a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266234} {:answer/id #uuid "637aa00c-7820-5986-bc25-d0f1c1c60ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{16}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266235} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f632af-384a-5deb-b568-a1149842d03b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-9y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266236} {:answer/id #uuid "b7030742-c2c3-5173-af94-f70f5b2194c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266237} {:answer/id #uuid "f7ab0699-725d-5f76-9383-44ba4ce46847" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-4(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb41fbf9-fb94-555b-a4f0-b34bf3bed3a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266234} :answer-field/id #uuid "16cdad93-3d3f-57e2-9a21-93652eb9eac8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266233}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6528ccf4-be62-5d95-9493-971f52ddc9ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-115513" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(4,0)$ and which passes through the point $(1,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,-4),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (1-4)^{2} & =4p(-4) \\\\ 9 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{9}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{9}{16})y \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =-\\frac{9}{4}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a34b79-65ff-5f7b-a8ee-5f19424fd9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94cd08a4-a20f-5788-befa-6f950c5bcc5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266241} {:answer/id #uuid "a15b7557-05f8-5b69-a5cf-54ecee47958f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266242} {:answer/id #uuid "00d1117f-9640-5c67-908a-231687919229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266243} {:answer/id #uuid "b70733b3-64eb-556d-8232-f4cd9d857df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266244} {:answer/id #uuid "57e8533c-bd8f-5901-a698-5889050bc0e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a15b7557-05f8-5b69-a5cf-54ecee47958f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266242} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b4587d-443a-5b2d-a578-a71dbdaa93f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266240}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2e48330-1eef-5bfd-9dfb-d4109d0a970c" :question/math-academy-id "q-11779" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561/assets/b09f63373bba1bb796f1832a4c7aa33ec220186e75bb84ccc79dcc46b662636e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is downward-opening has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-12).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-12),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-12$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-12) \\\\ 16 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a34b79-65ff-5f7b-a8ee-5f19424fd9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c687280c-e3db-5905-9405-a54945cca0cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266248} {:answer/id #uuid "2a4909a1-6aa1-5440-9aa7-09f8b412e4a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266249} {:answer/id #uuid "38781249-ac98-572d-8c2b-18ac9dda94fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266250} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6e93cb-9bbe-5ea3-826f-c17e6e71d7d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266251} {:answer/id #uuid "3c940e4f-86fd-5d75-a839-d26d41c3d189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c687280c-e3db-5905-9405-a54945cca0cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266248} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d7e046-79d9-5df0-9eca-2fca64ecb60f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266247}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0d1ed87b-e125-541f-8d8b-9155dc0504bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-11781" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561/assets/e588a9250583251ba2fd85b789eb3a856970fb6e7044289257a4ed97e3bbf1a0.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is upward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,6).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,6),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(6) \\\\ 16 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{2}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266253 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "281514e9-6e16-5b21-ba4e-503991d811c2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80532d30-77f7-5897-9ce1-c6bd231ae768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266255} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5d3168-f1c4-5830-ac31-732220c7ad71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266256} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f5d939-fbf4-5b60-aca3-257a491c93b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266257} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5666b8-cda1-5513-8c1b-1f33b5c0719d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266258} {:answer/id #uuid "33628d70-537d-561a-99e4-84d44b8d5c1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{2}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3f5d939-fbf4-5b60-aca3-257a491c93b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266257} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9580cf6-ac04-5e39-a226-e5d4a535039d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266254}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47a891f7-672e-5107-a414-bee92cb2f663" :question/math-academy-id "q-11782" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(4,-6).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-6) \\\\ 16 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{2}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{8}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "281514e9-6e16-5b21-ba4e-503991d811c2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca8bf18c-3fd6-5eff-a573-62b18802f685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} = \\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093939} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf945ca-a6f2-53cc-b431-4ae502771cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} = \\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093940} {:answer/id #uuid "014288f8-1dc5-57f5-bf2e-340c82ed75ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} =-\\frac{16}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093941} {:answer/id #uuid "27b50421-6fe9-531a-9c2b-582dcda5a75e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} =-\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093942} {:answer/id #uuid "29b8b38e-92bd-53ad-bbdb-bc3fad788345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} = \\frac{16}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29b8b38e-92bd-53ad-bbdb-bc3fad788345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} = \\frac{16}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093943} :answer-field/id #uuid "91ce8649-c05b-5558-a71e-b2314cd92961" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093938}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f57b6f5-a226-5a8e-b7f6-2247288e47e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-11788" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0, 0)$ and which passes through the point $(8, 12)$." :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(8,12),$ so we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=12$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {8}^{2} & =4p(12) \\\\ 64 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{4}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a34b79-65ff-5f7b-a8ee-5f19424fd9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6576999a-6db5-56af-9124-f7a3ee3137c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 175921862662