\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{5}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266267 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "281514e9-6e16-5b21-ba4e-503991d811c2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0cf56ca-2f98-58c3-8a40-d8047062c61d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266269} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fb6eab-a26e-5794-a3d0-b90227d6e6f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266270} {:answer/id #uuid "4394461f-da87-5470-9e98-7e1eb7b19c8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266271} {:answer/id #uuid "693d6b56-8fb2-5bb3-b1f8-15fc4ec38686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266272} {:answer/id #uuid "0b716a93-4acf-5130-9b69-a91f6e609567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266273}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b716a93-4acf-5130-9b69-a91f6e609567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266273} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a71da3b-5265-5443-bc9f-9bdddee6a94e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266268}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "638d02b8-a51f-5cc2-a74b-5fb529f64615" :question/math-academy-id "q-11792" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-5,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(-5,5),$ so we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=5$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-5)^{2} & =4p(5) \\\\ 25 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{5}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a133eff8-095f-5aa0-9e85-6559007ae130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 4)^{2} =-2(y + 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093975} {:answer/id #uuid "dd36c1e4-86ea-5eb2-9044-78158f64d3d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 4)^{2} =-2(y - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093976} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca8f448-4c66-58dc-aa7b-4b32fda4c1b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 4)^{2} = 4(y - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093977} {:answer/id #uuid "4fe4b474-45b6-5d22-be8b-f9016940ad1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 4)^{2} =-4(y - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093978} {:answer/id #uuid "6e29a32c-e893-546c-9dc5-b40fed2bb54a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 4)^{2} = 2(y - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd36c1e4-86ea-5eb2-9044-78158f64d3d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 4)^{2} =-2(y - 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093976} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d536e2a-4809-5333-8c8e-9f0d86e346cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093974}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a4d2719-1759-5386-919c-06942962bca0" :question/math-academy-id "q-11821" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1125/Source/Images/q-11821.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-4,4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}=4p(y-4).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(0,-4),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-4-4) \\\\ 16 & =-32p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-4) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})(y-4) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-2(y-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed27cbc8-2581-5751-83fb-f71d9985b2fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 3)^{2} =-7y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093987} {:answer/id #uuid "0db3ea8c-46fe-5ecc-81ec-7bc89cf2ad54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 3)^{2} = 3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093988} {:answer/id #uuid "1480bf6b-13a9-505b-99e5-e7dbbd47eb87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 3)^{2} =-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093989} {:answer/id #uuid "4a0a8001-db77-52a8-8498-58baff88c345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 3)^{2} = 7y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093990} {:answer/id #uuid "7efc445c-33e6-5ee4-bea9-d521e776ad0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} = 7(y - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a0a8001-db77-52a8-8498-58baff88c345" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 3)^{2} = 7y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093990} :answer-field/id #uuid "d899d713-4e6f-5864-9404-27fa8a819746" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093986}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74851aa4-2599-5d08-a6ea-51590faeb922" :question/math-academy-id "q-11831" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(3, 0)$ and which passes through the point $(-4, 7)$." :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(3,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y-0) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4py.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-4,7),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=7$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-4-3)^{2} & =4p(7) \\\\ 49 & =28p \\\\ p & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{7}{4})y \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =7y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "609eb2cf-b356-5ac6-8bc9-d6d4aee1ef47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-3(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266276} {:answer/id #uuid "c03a9a2b-767b-5f43-9517-494f82a6e0b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=7(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266277} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccaa4a8-e4e1-5348-85f8-6229d6f6f70c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-7(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266278} {:answer/id #uuid "2018be6b-4234-5633-b75a-6918b6dfe93e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=-7(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266279} {:answer/id #uuid "fb681254-8e38-5f7b-91d2-9717492bb5bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=3(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ccaa4a8-e4e1-5348-85f8-6229d6f6f70c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-7(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266278} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c878c30-8162-52d3-a874-55d3ed22eb9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266275}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32b923bd-666e-5667-b946-48b197182bfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-11833" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,1)$ and which passes through the point $(-6,-6).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(1,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-6,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=-6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (-6-1)^{2} & =4p(-6-1) \\\\ 49 & =-28p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{7}{4})(y-1) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-7(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2603a8d6-eb3e-524a-b6ae-2ec60dbc7779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266283} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0a1f2e-870c-5bed-bef6-605de135515a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266284} {:answer/id #uuid "8a36ac9b-46ae-518f-be4e-0c6a70fda25b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-5(y-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266285} {:answer/id #uuid "b52bf1e4-5961-5cf5-a528-ac08a59197ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266286} {:answer/id #uuid "02e6d837-77f5-5adf-adab-6b278893a260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266287}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "02e6d837-77f5-5adf-adab-6b278893a260" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-4)^{2}=-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266287} :answer-field/id #uuid "acbe6722-d902-59d5-bc30-2fefe97331d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266282}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36e0ed75-65a9-5980-8455-5068556a668b" :question/math-academy-id "q-11839" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561/assets/3dd6c2de764893839a2d3f817ab08faae32e1b2a09e031d4dfb897782917b8af.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(4,0).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4py.\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(9,-5),$ so we can substitute $x=9$ and $y=-5$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (9-4)^{2} & =4p(-5) \\\\ 25 & =-20p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4py \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{5}{4})y \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & =-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
